<p>At code <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">1</code>, creating the <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">CATextLayer</code> is the same as creating any other CALayer — nothing special to say. We set wrapping, alignment, truncation, etc.</p>

<p>At code <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">2</code>, let me focus on this <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">CAGradientLayer</code>.</p>

<p>At code <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">3</code> and <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">4</code>, we set the start and end directions of the mask gradient. (Using the bottom-left corner of the screen as origin 0,0 and the top-right corner as 1,1.)</p>

<blockquote>
  <p>If the start point is (0.0,0.5) and the end point is (1.0,0.5), it’s a horizontal gradient.
If the start point is (0.5,0) and the end point is (0.5,1), it’s a vertical gradient.
These two points determine the direction of the gradient.</p>
</blockquote>

